+  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Getting to Signed · execution workflow · capability map</div><h1>Manage the approval and signature process.</h1><div class="fam">execution workflow (live execution) · renamed from Negotiation 2026-07-07 · settled (position comparison + ledger)</div><p>Getting to Signed organizes legal, security, finance, and business approval workstreams. Show the customer request, proposed position, gap, owner, and next action.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.16b (the run-to-signed reconceive + rename, 2026-07-07) + B2B procurement research (SIG/CAIQ · SOC 2/ISO shortcut · liability caps · MAP). Settled design: getting-to-signed-faceoff-2026-07-07.html.</div></div>
+  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">Prioritize the issue currently blocking approval. Preserve the comparison of customer and seller positions, linked terms, document coverage, and dated mutual plan. Do not make unsupported claims about why deals are generally lost.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · The four disciplines</div><h2>Operating principles</h2><table><tr><th style="width:26px">#</th><th style="width:230px">Discipline</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td><span class="n">1</span></td><td><b>Every concession has a stated purpose</b></td><td>a give always asks for something back — never a reflex to “be reasonable”</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">2</span></td><td><b>You already hold the answers</b></td><td>a security questionnaire isn’t 150 blank lines — the coverage map shows how many your papers (SOC 2 · ISO 27001 · subprocessor list · pen-test · DPA) already answer</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">3</span></td><td><b>Keep stakeholders informed</b></td><td>a papers-ready strip + a plan-to-signed so nobody goes quiet</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">4</span></td><td><b>Terms are linked, not a list</b></td><td>a liability cap is worthless-or-fine depending on the indemnification ask — linked terms are settled together</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · The workstreams</div><h2>One ledger row each; the hottest is the current blocking issue</h2><div class="sd">Select the current blocking workstream from recorded status, rather than a fixed category order.</div><table><tr><th style="width:150px">Workstream</th><th>What it covers</th></tr><tr><td><b>Legal</b></td><td>liability cap + super-cap + indemnification, redlines</td></tr><tr><td><b>Security / InfoSec</b></td><td>the questionnaire + the coverage map + what still needs engineering</td></tr><tr><td><b>Finance</b></td><td>payment terms, prepay/discount trades, the signer</td></tr><tr><td><b>Business</b></td><td>keep the committee moving — champion + economic-buyer engagement, the plan-to-signed</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · What it offers</div><h2>The primitives</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The deal</b></td><td>From Deal Workspace — value, stage, days-to-target.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The committee</b></td><td>champion / economic buyer / new stakeholders, each with recorded engagement status and elapsed time since the last touch.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The papers-ready strip</b></td><td>SOC 2 · subprocessor list · pen-test · DPA.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The current blocking issue</b></td><td>their side · the gap · your proposed position + limits and proposed exchanges + “send the counter-position” + “ask an advisor with relevant experience.”</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The positions ledger</b></td><td>per-workstream row: their ask → your proposed position → status (blocking / clearing / to-do / at-risk); each row drills to its depth in place.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The coverage map (security)</b></td><td>a bar of answered-by-your-papers vs still-open, with which paper answers how many, and how many open ones need engineering.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The payment-terms trade set</b></td><td>net-60 flat · net-30 for a cut · % off for annual upfront payment.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The plan-to-signed</b></td><td>the mutual dated signature process, co-owned with the champion.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The “we won’t repeat this” log</b></td><td>feeds loss patterns.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="sd">Held for the <b>Pro gate</b>: RFP-response handling (formal RFP/RFI intake, question-splitting, drafted answers) — real and wanted, but scoped to a later version, not the first-ship room.</div><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li>deal from <b>Deal Workspace</b> (value, stage, decision architecture)</li><li>the pilot’s evidence from <b>Pilot Desk</b> (leverage in a terms conversation)</li><li>an advisor from <b>Call in a Favor</b> (a network request relevant to a blocked approval)</li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>workstream outcomes + the plan-to-signed → <b>Deal Workspace</b></li><li>loss-pattern feedback → <b>Future Autopsy</b> (lost in procurement, before terms?)</li><li>a stuck workstream → <b>Call in a Favor</b> (request relevant help for the blocked approval)</li><li>patterns → <b>Founding GTM</b> §6 (why we win) + §4 (where deals leak)</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">6 · Language — plain, no procurement jargon</div><h2>The teardown</h2><div class="sd">The seller is a first-timer facing their first enterprise legal/security gauntlet. Security frameworks (SOC 2, ISO 27001, SIG, DPA, pen-test) are named plainly because they’re the real objects — but each is framed as a paper the seller holds (“answers 88 of the questions”), never an acronym to decode.</div><table data-copy-context="deprecated-example"><tr><th style="width:230px">Banned on the face</th><th>Say instead</th></tr><tr><td>“counterparty”</td><td>their side / their legal / their security team</td></tr><tr><td>“concession ladder”</td><td>what you can trade</td></tr><tr><td>“redline” (unless taught)</td><td>the change they want to the contract</td></tr><tr><td>the economic buyer</td><td>the person who signs off</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">7 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>The signature-process model (the deal isn’t won at yes)</li><li>The current blocking issue on the blocking workstream (their side ⟷ your proposed position, always the one actually blocking)</li><li>Every-concession-is-a-trade</li><li>You already hold the answers (the coverage map, never a 150-line data-entry chore)</li><li>Stakeholder communication, recorded engagement, and a dated signature plan</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Not a pricing calculator</li><li>Not a rehearsal booth</li><li>Never soften into “negotiation tips”</li><li>RFP tooling is held for the Pro gate — not in the base room</li></ul></div></div></div>
