 <div class="map"><h2>4. What this room is for and how it works</h2><p>The room's capability map: its purpose, every part and what it does, what flows in from and out to other rooms, and what it must never become.</p><div class="wrap">
-  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Call in a Favor · Live Instrument · capability map</div><h1>Mobilize the people in your corner to move a stuck deal.</h1><div class="fam">Live Instrument (live execution) · renamed from Advisor Deploy 2026-07-06 · settled (the relay)</div><p>Mobilize the people in your corner — investors, advisors, happy customers — to move a stuck deal you can’t move alone. Prepare one backchannel ask before you spend that trust, route it, log it, watch it come back as deal movement. A founder’s real unfair advantage is their network; this room makes that deliberate instead of ad-hoc. <b>Deal-first</b>: the room opens on the stuck deals that need outside help.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.16 (renamed + deal-first + plain-language teardown, 2026-07-06) + shipped src/advisor-deploy. Settled design: call-in-a-favor-toprail-2026-07-06.html.</div></div>
-  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">Know who you can call on, match the right person to the right deal, pick the right ask for the deal’s state, write the exact message AND the note they forward to the buyer — don’t spend trust on a vague ask, don’t over-tap the same person, and close every loop. The favor is a <b>relay</b>: you → your person → the buyer.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · The favor as a hand-off</div><h2>You → your person → the buyer</h2><div class="sd">The room writes both halves. The discipline is making the person’s half easy to say yes to — the easier their lift, the more likely they help, and the less you lean on a favor you may need again.</div><table><tr><th style="width:180px">Note</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td><b>Your message</b></td><td>what you send your person</td></tr><tr><td><b>The forwardable note</b></td><td>the short note they forward to the buyer — they should barely have to change it</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · The ten asks</div><h2>What you’re asking for</h2><div class="sd">Ten plain situations, matched to the deal’s state.</div><ul class="plain"><li>open the door · get an exec involved · nudge a stalled pilot · unstick procurement · reset the comparison · lost your champion · save it from a budget freeze · signal you’re a serious bet · get a customer to vouch · warm up a renewal</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · What it offers</div><h2>The primitives</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The deal</b></td><td>From Deal Workspace — the stuck deal the favor moves.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Who to ask</b></td><td>Your people — investors / advisors / happy customers, each with what they know + which accounts they carry weight with.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>What you’re asking for</b></td><td>The ten plain situations (above).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The two notes</b></td><td>Your message + the forwardable note.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The ready check</b></td><td>“Is this ask small and specific enough to send?”</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The don’t-over-ask guard</b></td><td>A gentle “second ask in a short window — open with a thank-you” when you’ve asked that person recently.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The stuck-deal queue</b></td><td>Top rail, most-stuck first + a coverage read (deals with no one who could put in a word → line someone up).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Log + loop-close</b></td><td>The favor comes back on the deal as movement or a clear no.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li>deal from <b>Deal Workspace</b></li><li>the pilot’s evidence from <b>Pilot Desk</b></li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>the favor’s effect on the deal → <b>Deal Workspace</b> · <b>Dashboard</b> · <b>Handoff</b></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">6 · Language — plain, no insider jargon</div><h2>The teardown</h2><div class="sd">The old room was jargon-heavy — all reworded on the face. Code enum keys may stay; the user-facing surface is clean.</div><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Banned on the face</th><th>Say instead</th></tr><tr><td>“deploy” (people aren’t troops)</td><td>call in a favor</td></tr><tr><td>“spend-read”</td><td>“is this ask ready to send?”</td></tr><tr><td>rolodex / carriers</td><td>your people / who’s in your corner</td></tr><tr><td>“ask-moment”</td><td>what you need help with</td></tr><tr><td>the economic buyer</td><td>the person who signs off</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">7 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>The deal-linked consequence (a favor is not a side log — it moves a specific deal)</li><li>The don’t-waste-trust discipline (ready check + don’t-over-ask)</li><li>The hand-off craft (both notes, easy to say yes to)</li><li>Closing the loop</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Not a side log</li><li>Not a contact CRM</li><li>Not a mass-intro tool</li><li>Not a networking rolodex</li></ul></div></div></div>
+<div class="callout blue"><div class="ct">Language and capability review · 2026-11-02</div><div class="cb">Use the current source and docs/language-remediation-2026 for implementation. Earlier design references below preserve history; they do not authorize deprecated customer language or capabilities that the source does not implement. Keep stable internal identifiers separate from display wording. Distinguish evidence, assessment, forecast, and recommendation.</div></div>
+  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Call in a Favor · execution workflow · capability map</div><h1>Request specific help from your network.</h1><div class="fam">execution workflow (live execution) · renamed from Advisor Deploy 2026-07-06 · settled (network-assistance workflow)</div><p>Call in a Favor links a deal to a person who may help, prepares the request and forwardable note, and records the outcome. Existing relationships do not guarantee influence or buyer cooperation.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.16 (renamed + deal-first + plain-language teardown, 2026-07-06) + shipped src/advisor-deploy. Settled design: call-in-a-favor-toprail-2026-07-06.html.</div></div>
+  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">Keep the request specific, review recent requests to the same person, and record introductions, information, declines, or other actual outcomes. Preserve deal linkage and both message drafts.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · The favor as a hand-off</div><h2>You → your person → the buyer</h2><div class="sd">The room writes both halves. The discipline is making the person’s half easy to say yes to — the easier their lift, the more likely they help, and the less you lean on a favor you may need again.</div><table><tr><th style="width:180px">Note</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td><b>Your message</b></td><td>what you send your person</td></tr><tr><td><b>The forwardable note</b></td><td>the short note they forward to the buyer — they should barely have to change it</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · The ten asks</div><h2>What you’re asking for</h2><div class="sd">Ten plain situations, matched to the deal’s state.</div><ul class="plain"><li>open the door · get an exec involved · nudge a stalled pilot · unstick procurement · reset the comparison · lost your champion · save it from a budget freeze · signal you’re a serious bet · get a customer to vouch · warm up a renewal</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · What it offers</div><h2>The primitives</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The deal</b></td><td>From Deal Workspace — the stuck deal the favor moves.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Who to ask</b></td><td>Your people — investors / advisors / happy customers, each with what they know + which accounts they carry weight with.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>What you’re asking for</b></td><td>The ten plain situations (above).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The two notes</b></td><td>Your message + the forwardable note.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The ready check</b></td><td>“Is this ask small and specific enough to send?”</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The don’t-over-ask guard</b></td><td>A gentle “second ask in a short window — open with a thank-you” when you’ve asked that person recently.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The stuck-deal queue</b></td><td>Top rail, most-stuck first + a coverage read (deals with no one who could put in a word → line someone up).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Log + loop-close</b></td><td>Record the request outcome on the linked deal, including declines and no response.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li>deal from <b>Deal Workspace</b></li><li>the pilot’s evidence from <b>Pilot Desk</b></li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>the favor’s effect on the deal → <b>Deal Workspace</b> · <b>Dashboard</b> · <b>Handoff</b></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">6 · Language — plain, no insider jargon</div><h2>The teardown</h2><div class="sd">The old room was jargon-heavy — all reworded on the face. Code enum keys may stay; the user-facing surface is clean.</div><table data-copy-context="deprecated-example"><tr><th style="width:230px">Banned on the face</th><th>Say instead</th></tr><tr><td>“deploy” (people aren’t troops)</td><td>call in a favor</td></tr><tr><td>“spend-read”</td><td>“is this ask ready to send?”</td></tr><tr><td>rolodex / carriers</td><td>your people / who’s in your corner</td></tr><tr><td>“ask-moment”</td><td>what you need help with</td></tr><tr><td>the economic buyer</td><td>the person who signs off</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">7 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" sty